#!/usr/bin/env node
/*
 * tools/githooks/merge-gate -- the merge-gate dispatcher. Check-register row CHK-38.
 * Installed at 2.20 by The Systems Engineer, 2026-08-28.
 *
 * WHY. CHK-01 and CHK-04 have named the trigger `merge-gate` since 1.13 and nothing installed a
 * dispatcher for it. Two BLOCKING rows fired on nothing, which made them decorative on the one
 * day they matter, and 2.5 -- the corpus merge -- is that day.
 *
 * MERGE-GATE IS NOT A GIT EVENT, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. On git
 * 2.55.0.windows.1, `git hook run merge-gate` exits 1 with `unknown hook event 'merge-gate'; use
 * --allow-unknown-hook-name to allow non-native hook names`. So the invocation is
 *
 *     git hook run --allow-unknown-hook-name merge-gate
 *
 * and check_register.md's TRIGGERS table records that literal, because a dispatcher reachable
 * only by a flag nobody wrote down is a dispatcher nobody runs. Running the file directly --
 * `node tools/githooks/merge-gate` -- also works and is the same code path; the hook-run form is
 * preferred because it goes through git's own resolver, which is the whole reason E1's remedy was
 * replaced (asserting core.hooksPath proves nothing: git exits 0 against a nonexistent or empty
 * directory and fires nothing either way).
 *
 * WHAT IT DISPATCHES. Every row whose invoked_by names merge-gate, in row order, through the
 * shared engine in ./dispatch.js. Nothing about the dispatch rule is written twice; see that file
 * for why that mattered enough to move it.
 *
 * HAND-OPERATED IS NOT UNWIRED, AND THE 1.13 RULING STANDS. check_register.md section 5.2 ruled
 * merge-gate KEPT and declared hand-operated: its operator is the agent executing 2.5. That
 * ruling was about WHO PULLS THE TRIGGER and it is unchanged. This file is WHAT THE TRIGGER
 * DOES. The two were conflated in the 2.20 brief -- "nothing installs a dispatcher" was read as
 * "the trigger is unwired" -- and they are different failures with different fixes. An operator
 * with no mechanism and a mechanism with no operator are both broken; until today this trigger
 * was the first, and the TRIGGERS table said so honestly.
 *
 * THE 100644 AND CRLF TRAPS APPLY TO THIS FILE EXACTLY AS THEY APPLY TO pre-commit. See that
 * file's header and .gitattributes. `git ls-files -s tools/githooks/merge-gate` must report
 * 100755.
 *
 * NO SELF-INVOCATION, and the guard for it is now shared. A row dispatched here that shells out
 * to any trigger re-enters dispatch.js at depth 1 and is refused with the chain printed. Adding
 * this second trigger is what made a per-trigger guard insufficient, and the guard was
 * generalised in the same edit rather than a later one.
 */

'use strict';

const { dispatch } = require('./dispatch.js');

process.exit(dispatch({ trigger: 'merge-gate', rowId: 'CHK-38 merge-gate dispatcher:' }));
